---
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro'
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro'
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const repo = 'https://github.com/DontAskForAnything/tabhangar'
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy policy — TabHangar"
  description="TabHangar keeps your data in your browser or on a server you run. No analytics, no ads, no tracking."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16 sm:py-24">
    <article class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2 [&_a]:font-medium [&_a]:text-accent-ink [&_a:hover]:underline [&_h2]:mt-12 [&_h2]:mb-3 [&_h2]:text-[22px] [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-[-0.02em] [&_h2]:text-ink [&_li]:mt-1.5 [&_p]:mt-4 [&_ul]:mt-4 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-6">
      <h1 class="text-[38px] leading-tight font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-[46px]">
        Privacy policy
      </h1>
      <p class="text-[14px] text-ink-3">Last updated: September 2026</p>

      <p>
        TabHangar is an open-source browser extension and an optional sync server you can run
        yourself. This policy explains what happens with your data. In short: it stays with you.
      </p>

      <h2>What the extension stores</h2>
      <p>
        The things you save — page titles and addresses, notes, to-dos, images, reminders — and your
        settings are stored in your browser's local storage (IndexedDB). They never leave your
        browser unless you turn on sync with a server you choose.
      </p>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. TabHangar has no analytics, no advertising, no tracking and no crash reporting.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>We do not sell, share or transfer any data to third parties.</li>
        <li>There are no TabHangar accounts or servers run by us.</li>
        <li>Your data is not used for any purpose other than showing your boards to you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Network requests</h2>
      <p>
        To show what you saved, the extension loads website icons and images from the websites they
        belong to. Website icons are taken from the browser's own icon cache when possible. No other
        requests are made, except to your sync server if you connect one.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Tabs and tab groups</strong> — to list your open tabs and save the ones you
          choose.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Context menus, active tab and scripting</strong> — to save the page you are on,
          only after you click the menu, the toolbar button or the shortcut. The extension has no
          access to websites otherwise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Storage</strong> — to keep your boards on your device.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Alarms and notifications</strong> — to show reminders you set.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bookmarks and top sites</strong> (optional) — only when you search bookmarks or
          import them; read locally, never sent anywhere.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Optional sync</h2>
      <p>
        If you enter a server address in Settings → Account & sync, your boards are sent to that
        server so your other browsers can download them. The server is software you (or someone you
        trust) run; it stores your username, a scrypt hash of your password, hashed session tokens
        and the data you sync. Whoever runs the server is responsible for it. You can sign out at
        any time; your data stays in the browser.
      </p>

      <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>In the extension: Settings → Data → Delete all data, or uninstall the extension.</li>
        <li>On a sync server: the server owner can delete your account and all of its data.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>TabHangar is not directed at children and does not knowingly collect any information.</p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the new version will be published on this page and in the
        <a href={repo}>project repository</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions are welcome as an issue in the <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>GitHub repository</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
